INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('tgweb_english', '長文問題', 'ホーウィックのニット産業', 'Hawick, a town in the Scottish Borders, has been closely associated with knitwear production since the late 18th century. The surrounding hills provided grazing land for sheep with especially fine wool, and the River Teviot supplied the clean, soft water needed for washing and dyeing fibers. These natural advantages, combined with the arrival of stocking-frame knitting machines around 1771, allowed Hawick to develop a reputation for high-quality knitted garments that continues today.

Fiona Elliot runs one of the last family-owned knitting mills in Hawick. She specializes in cashmere sweaters, using fibers combed rather than sheared from goats to avoid harming the animals. Elliot learned to operate traditional knitting frames from her mother, who ran the mill before her. When Elliot took over the business in 2003, Hawick was home to about a dozen mills of similar size. Only three, including hers, are still operating today.

Elliot often mentions Robert Pringle, who founded a knitwear company in Hawick in 1815. Before Pringle’s innovations, sweaters were typically knitted in a single piece and offered little variety in shape. Pringle introduced techniques for shaping garments during knitting rather than cutting and sewing pieces afterward, which reduced waste and produced a better fit. This method became standard practice throughout the town and helped establish Hawick’s knitwear as internationally recognized.

A framed photograph of Pringle’s original mill hangs above Elliot’s workbench. Like Pringle, she believes that comfort and craftsmanship should never be sacrificed for speed. Over the years, Elliot has mentored young knitters through a local training program. In 2021, a graduate of that program helped her design a limited collection that was featured in a regional fashion exhibition, bringing new attention to her mill. Elliot hopes to eventually convert part of her mill into a small museum, where visitors can see historic knitting frames and learn about the town’s textile history.

【設問】
What natural advantage helped Hawick become known for knitwear?', NULL, '["Nearby forests provided wood for knitting frames.","The area had fine wool and clean water for processing it.","The town’s climate allowed wool to be dyed outdoors year-round.","Local rivers were used to power large factories."]', 1, '["第1段落：良質な羊毛ときれいな水がニット産業を支えた。"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（TG-WEB新型英語: 長文問題）', 902);
