INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('tgweb_english', '長文問題', '美濃の和紙づくり', 'The town of Mino, in Japan’s Gifu Prefecture, has produced handmade paper, known as washi, for more than 1,300 years. The clear water of the nearby Nagara River and the local cultivation of kozo, a type of mulberry tree whose fibers are used in papermaking, made the area especially suited to the craft. Because Mino paper was known for its strength and smooth texture, it was historically used for important documents, and its production techniques were designated as a UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2014.

Kenji Sato is one of the few remaining certified washi artisans in Mino. He harvests kozo by hand each winter, then boils, beats, and dissolves the fibers before forming sheets using a wooden frame and a bamboo screen, a process that can take an entire day for a single batch. Sato began his training in 1988, when Mino had roughly 30 workshops producing washi by traditional methods. Today, fewer than ten such workshops remain.

Sato often credits Sadanobu Imai, a papermaker active in the 1890s, as an inspiration. At that time, most washi was made only in plain white sheets. Imai developed methods for mixing natural plant dyes into the pulp itself, creating paper in soft colors without weakening its fibers. His colored washi became popular for lanterns, folding screens, and decorative crafts, expanding the uses of the paper beyond writing and printing.

In his workshop, Sato displays a sample of Imai’s colored paper, carefully preserved under glass. Like Imai, Sato believes that washi should serve both practical and artistic purposes. He has taught the craft to a number of apprentices, and in 2022, one of them arranged for Sato’s work to be featured in an exhibition at a regional craft museum. This exposure brought new customers to his workshop. Sato now hopes to build a small studio where school groups can observe papermaking firsthand, ensuring that younger generations understand the effort behind each sheet.

【設問】
What made the area around Mino especially suited to papermaking?', NULL, '["Its dry climate preserved paper for centuries.","It had clear river water and locally grown kozo trees.","It was close to Japan’s major cities for easy trade.","Local temples required large amounts of paper."]', 1, '["第1段落：清流と地元産の楮（こうぞ）が適していた。"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（TG-WEB新型英語: 長文問題）', 903);
