INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('tgweb_english', '長文問題', 'プレスコットの時計職人', 'Prescot, a market town in the northwest of England, became a center of clockmaking during the 17th and 18th centuries. Its location near iron and coal deposits gave local workers easy access to the raw materials needed for tools and machine parts, while its distance from London allowed smaller workshops to operate without competing directly against the capital’s larger clockmaking firms. By the mid-1700s, Prescot supplied clock and watch components to makers throughout Britain.

Thomas Yardley continues this tradition at his small workshop in the town center. He specializes in hand-finishing brass movements, the internal mechanisms that power mechanical clocks, using tools passed down from his father. Yardley began his apprenticeship in 1982, when Prescot still had about a dozen workshops producing clock components. Today, his is one of only two that remain in operation.

Yardley often speaks of Margaret Ashworth, a toolmaker who established a workshop in Prescot in 1832. Before her innovations, clock components were shaped almost entirely by hand, a slow process prone to small inconsistencies. Ashworth developed precision cutting tools that allowed components to be produced with much greater accuracy and in less time. Her tools were soon adopted by workshops throughout the town, greatly increasing Prescot’s output without sacrificing quality.

In his workshop, Yardley keeps one of Ashworth’s original cutting tools, still functional after nearly two centuries. Like Ashworth, he believes that precision and craftsmanship are not opposing goals but complementary ones. Yardley has trained a small number of apprentices over the years, and in 2018, one of them helped him set up a workshop website that attracted collectors from across Europe. Yardley now hopes to establish a small workshop museum, where visitors can see antique tools and learn how clock components were traditionally made.

【設問】
What advantage did Prescot have for clockmaking?', NULL, '["It had access to iron and coal and was far enough from London to allow small workshops to thrive.","It was the only town in England permitted to produce clocks.","Its cold climate helped preserve metal tools.","It had a large river used to power all local workshops."]', 0, '["第1段落：鉄・石炭への近さとロンドンからの距離が小規模工房の存続を助けた。"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（TG-WEB新型英語: 長文問題）', 905);
