Meera Tiwari manages a small weaving workshop that has stayed in her family for four generations. She still uses a traditional wooden loom, tying each knot by hand according to patterns that can take months to complete for a single large carpet. Tiwari began weaving under her mother’s guidance in 1996, when their neighborhood had about 30 workshops producing hand-knotted carpets. Today, only a dozen remain, as many former weavers now work in factories using power looms instead.

Tiwari frequently mentions Ramesh Yadav, a weaver who established a workshop in Bhadohi in 1901. Before his time, most carpets in the region featured a limited set of repeating geometric patterns. Yadav began incorporating detailed floral and animal motifs inspired by regional textile art, requiring far greater skill and patience from weavers but producing carpets considered more visually striking. His designs became widely admired and were soon copied by workshops throughout the town.

A carpet fragment believed to have been woven in Yadav’s original workshop hangs on Tiwari’s wall. Like Yadav, she believes that patience and attention to detail cannot be replaced by speed. Tiwari has trained many young weavers over the years, and in 2020, a former student helped her connect with an international fair-trade organization that now sells her carpets abroad. Tiwari hopes to eventually open a small training center where local children can learn hand-knotting skills, ensuring the craft is not lost as power looms become more common.

【設問】
Ramesh Yadav changed carpet design in Bhadohi by', NULL, '["introducing power looms to the region.","adding intricate plant and creature imagery drawn from local textile traditions.","limiting patterns to simple geometric shapes.","establishing the first carpet school in India."]', 1, '["第3段落：Yadavは花や動物をモチーフにした精緻なデザインを導入。"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（TG-WEB新型英語: 長文問題）', 908);
